          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "66"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.161,
            0.785,
            0.271,
            0.852
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 2,
              "bbox": [
                0.161,
                0.785,
                0.271,
                0.852
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_5_image_2.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.085,
            0.883,
            0.247
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A freely suspended magnet comes to rest along the north-south direction. The end of the magnet that points towards north direction is called the North-seeking pole or the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "North pole of the magnet",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". The other end that points towards the South direction is called the South-seeking pole or the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "South pole of the magnet",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". A freely suspended magnet rests along the north-south direction because our Earth itself behaves like a giant magnet."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.248,
            0.883,
            0.37
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Repeat this activity with a small iron bar in place of the bar magnet. What do you observe? Does it always rest along north-south direction? It does not. It can rest along any direction. This implies that only magnets rest along north-south direction. This activity provides us with a way to test whether a piece of metal is a magnet or not."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.255,
            0.37,
            0.883,
            0.43
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The property of a freely suspended magnet to always rest along the north-south direction is used to find directions. Based on this, a small device called a magnetic compass was"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.101,
            0.435,
            0.355,
            0.631
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 6,
              "bbox": [
                0.101,
                0.435,
                0.355,
                0.631
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_5_image_6.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_footnote",
              "index": 7,
              "bbox": [
                0.148,
                0.638,
                0.308,
                0.671
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 4.6: A magnetic compass"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.361,
            0.431,
            0.883,
            0.513
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "developed in olden days for finding directions. It has a magnet in the shape of a needle which can rotate freely (Fig. 4.6). The needle of a magnetic compass indicates the north-south direction."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.361,
            0.514,
            0.883,
            0.657
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The compass is kept at the place where we wish to know the directions. After some time, the needle comes to rest in the north-south direction. The compass box is then gently rotated until the north and south marked on the dial are aligned with the needle. Now all directions at that place are as indicated on the dial."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.167,
            0.701,
            0.673,
            0.783
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A magnetic compass is usually a small circular box with a transparent cover on it, as shown in Fig. 4.6. The magnet, in the shape of a needle, is mounted on a pin standing on the bottom of the box. This"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.856,
            0.273,
            0.898
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "More to know!",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.714,
            0.695,
            0.845,
            0.766
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "How can we make our own magnetic compass?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.284,
            0.783,
            0.744,
            0.906
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "needle is balanced on the pin in such a manner that it can move around this point easily, that is, it can rotate freely. The end of the needle which rests in the North direction is usually painted red. Below the needle, there is a dial with directions marked on it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
